#
# prima-materia — chaos magic
#
# Order of the file: the tradition and its works; the quantum model of the
# brain, which is what the file was opened against; and the harvest of the
# pages that carry it, which name divination and enchantment as the measure
# of magic, state a condition under which divination fails, and rank the
# carriers of a magical link; then Sherwin, The Book of Results, harvested
# complete under prima-materia#893, in the order of the work's own terms:
# capacities and states, the model, practices, yieldings, prerequisites,
# cautions, names, the dispute.
#
# Opened out of order, against the second clause of the sentence held at
# prima-materia#888, which credits chaos magicians with mapping a receiver
# model onto neural microtubules. The two works opened carry a quantum model
# of the brain and no microtubule; the searches stand in
# examinations/searches-chaos-magic.ttl. Of the corpus, The Book of Results
# is harvested; the other works are not.
#

pmt:ChaosMagic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Chaos magic"@en, "Chaosmagie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The late twentieth century transmission treating belief as a tool to be adopted and discarded, and holding that the symbolic system employed is arbitrary if the technique is sound."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim that content is interchangeable and only technique carries effect is directly examinable, and if it held it would reorganise every convergence question the project asks."@en ;
    skos:note "Read in this run at the wording, on 2026-10-22. The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008, first edition, the date on the copyright page), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, General Metadynamics and magic, pp. 77-82, in a full-text layer whose running heads carry the page; and The Octavo: A Sorcerer-Scientist's Grimoire, Roundworld Edition (Mandrake of Oxford, 2011, first edition), at Magical Ritual part 1, Centring, pp. 33-35, and chap. 4, The Spells of Subtle Magic, pp. 77-79. The rest of both works, and Liber Null and Psychonaut, Liber Kaos, PsyberMagick, Condensed Chaos and Prime Chaos, were searched by term only, as examinations/searches-chaos-magic.ttl states."@en ;
    skos:note "The contact route is documented from inside the corpus: The Apophenion cites Liber Kaos, Ramsey Dukes and Penrose's Road to Reality in its notes, and Carroll's short essay Chaoism and Chaos Magic calls Bohr and Spare his saints; The Octavo takes its centring term Gnosis from western esotericism and equates it with dhyana and samadhi in the same sentence. Agreement with Thelema, the Typhonian corpus or the yogic material is reception."@en ;
    skos:note "Read complete on 2026-10-23: Sherwin, The Book of Results, in an edition after 1991 with a foreword by Carroll, cited by chapter and heading for want of page numbers in the full-text layer; harvested under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction passes."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "Spare, The Book of Pleasure; Carroll, Liber Null and Psychonaut; Carroll, Liber Kaos; Carroll, PsyberMagick; Carroll, The Apophenion; Carroll, The Octavo; Hine, Condensed Chaos; Hine, Prime Chaos" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut, pmw:LiberKaos, pmw:PsyberMagick, pmw:TheApophenion, pmw:TheOctavo, pmw:CondensedChaos, pmw:PrimeChaos, pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, pp. 79-81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion .

pmw:LiberNullAndPsychonaut a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Null and Psychonaut"@en, "Liber Null and Psychonaut"@de ;
    skos:note "Carroll. York Beach: Samuel Weiser, 1987, two earlier booklets in one volume. Reachable as a full text; searched by term only, on 2026-10-21 and again on 2026-10-22, not opened."@en .

pmw:LiberKaos a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Liber Kaos"@en, "Liber Kaos"@de ;
    skos:note "Carroll. York Beach: Weiser, 1992. Searched by term on 2026-10-21 in the revised Weiser edition of 2023 and on 2026-10-22 in two further full-text layers of the text; one upload of 1992 carries only an epub with a damaged chapter file. Not opened."@en .

pmw:PsyberMagick a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "PsyberMagick: Advanced Ideas in Chaos Magic"@en, "PsyberMagick"@de ;
    skos:note "Carroll. Tempe: New Falcon, 1995, reissued 1997. The library scan is lending-restricted; an upload of some eighty thousand characters without imprint was searched by term only on 2026-10-22, not opened."@en .

pmw:TheApophenion a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm"@en, "The Apophenion"@de ;
    skos:note "Carroll. Mandrake of Oxford, 2008. Opened on 2026-10-22 at chap. 5, part 3, pp. 77-82; the rest searched by term. Names Penrose three times: as the author of a sentence on time, in the bibliography for The Road to Reality, and in the index; never for microtubules."@en .

pmw:TheOctavo a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Octavo: A Sorcerer-Scientist's Grimoire"@en, "The Octavo"@de ;
    skos:note "Carroll. The Roundworld Edition, Mandrake of Oxford, 2011, is the one read; its copyright page calls it a first edition. Opened on 2026-10-22 at the centring and at chap. 4; the rest searched by term."@en .

pmw:CondensedChaos a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Condensed Chaos: An Introduction to Chaos Magic"@en, "Condensed Chaos"@de ;
    skos:note "Hine. Tempe: New Falcon, 1995. Reachable as a full text; searched by term only on 2026-10-22, not opened."@en .

pmw:PrimeChaos a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Prime Chaos: Adventures in Chaos Magic"@en, "Prime Chaos"@de ;
    skos:note "Hine. London: Chaos International, 1993; Tempe: New Falcon, 1999. The library scan of 1999 is lending-restricted; a second full-text layer was searched by term only on 2026-10-22, not opened."@en .

#
# The quantum model of the brain
#

pmc:ChaosMagicCerebrumQuanticum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The brain as a chaotic analogue quantum computer, and magic as quantum effects it lets through"@en, "Das Gehirn als chaotischer analoger Quantencomputer, und Magie als Quanteneffekte, die es durchlässt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The model the work proposes for why magic works at all. Once spirits, transcendental agencies and aethers are set aside, only quantum ideas are said to remain as models, so the brain must let some quantum effects show at the macroscopic level: besides a chaotic analogue computer it must be, to some degree, a chaotic analogue quantum computer. Two states are mapped onto it: the void reached by stilling the mind or by distraction is said to let parts of the brain relax into superposition, out of which inspiration and sometimes divination collapse; gnosis, reached by extreme excitation or extreme focus of the nervous system, is said to correspond to coherence, wide areas of the brain showing the same activity while others are inhibited."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the place in the chaos-magical corpus nearest to the second clause of prima-materia#888, and it is entered so that the distance is on the page. The quantum is put into the brain as a whole and into states of it; no structure inside the neurone is named, the microtubule does not occur in the work, and Penrose is named only for time. The receiver is not a theme: the brain is a computer whose quantum part lets the magician's own states act, not an organ that picks up signals from elsewhere."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, General Metadynamics and magic, pp. 79-81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCohaerentiaNeurologica a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis as neurological quantum coherence, and the synapse as the damper of quantum thought"@en, "Gnosis als neurologische Quantenkohärenz, und die Synapse als Dämpfer des Quantendenkens"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two passages of the later work. At the centring, the single-pointed state called gnosis, equated with the dhyana of raja yoga and with samadhi, is offered in western terms as a neurological quantum coherence in which active parts of the brain enter a single quantum state or approach one. In the chapter on subtle magic, an enlarged indeterminacy is said to supply enough energy each second to drive some ten thousand neurones, so that the problem is no longer to find quantum activity in the brain but to explain why it does not overwhelm us; the damping is placed in the chemical synapses, which sufficient hallucinogens are said to switch off, after which thoughts arrive unbidden every second. Magicians are said to want a selective use of this function, for seeking visions or for implanting thoughts in the minds of others."@en ;
    skos:note "Where the work places the quantum event it names the synapse, not the microtubule, and it says so in the same paragraph that names the hallucinogen as the test. The nearest neurological site in the corpus to the sentence of prima-materia#888 is therefore a different one from the sentence's, and the sentence reads less as a garbled report of this passage than as the substitution of a better-known theory for it."@en ;
    skos:note "The implanting of thoughts in another mind is a capacity, named here in passing; the procedure for it stands in the later chapters of the work, which were not read."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheOctavo ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Octavo: A Sorcerer-Scientist's Grimoire, Roundworld Edition (Mandrake of Oxford, 2011), Magical Ritual part 1, Centring, p. 34, and chap. 4, The Spells of Subtle Magic, pp. 78-79; read in a full-text layer of that edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSilentiumInternum a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic succeeds only for one who can switch off the internal dialogue"@en, "Magie gelingt nur, wer den inneren Dialog abschalten kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition with which the centring opens: the chattering mind is the main obstacle to magical intent, and only those who can learn to switch off the internal dialogue can succeed with magic, since every thought or desire provokes its negation as a doubt or a further thought; only unwavering intent serves, even if held for a few minutes."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicQuinqueOperationes ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicCohaerentiaNeurologica ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without the stilled dialogue the intent is met at once by its own doubt, and the work states that magic does not succeed." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheOctavo ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Octavo: A Sorcerer-Scientist's Grimoire, Roundworld Edition (Mandrake of Oxford, 2011), Magical Ritual part 1, Centring, pp. 33-34; read in a full-text layer of that edition" .

#
# The harvest of pp. 77-82 of The Apophenion
#

pmc:ChaosMagicQuinqueOperationes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Five operations of magic, of which divination and enchantment are the measure"@en, "Fünf Operationen der Magie, deren Maß Divination und Verzauberung sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "The ideas of magic are reduced to five: divination, revealing information by non-ordinary means; enchantment, encouraging desired events by non-ordinary means; evocation, commanding spirits to help with either; invocation, identification with or possession by spirits for the same ends; and illumination, special knowledge and powers that reduce to the first two. Divination and enchantment are therefore called the basic measure of magic, the other three being psychological supports. The practices that give the best results are said to remain uncertain, ad hoc and rule of thumb, refined toward a toolbox only by the hypotheses of gnosis and of sleight of mind."@en ;
    skos:note "The admission that the practices remain rule of thumb stands in the same paragraph as the claim, and it is the transmission's own statement about how far its procedures are fixed."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, pp. 77-78; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDivinatioPerStatumFuturum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Divination as access to a future state of one's own brain, and the experiment in which it fails"@en, "Divination als Zugriff auf einen künftigen Zustand des eigenen Gehirns, und der Versuch, in dem sie scheitert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The mechanism the work gives for divination: the brain may keep superposed states entangled with its own past and future states, so that the diviner reaches some future state of his own brain in which the answer is known. From this it draws two consequences. Divination experiments in which the diviners will never learn whether they divined correctly are said usually to fail badly; and since superposition and entanglement decay over time, divinations are said to work best over short spans, the author adding that most of his own premonitions come a few minutes before the event. Some magicians are said to visualise themselves at the future time when the answer is known and to resolve to send it back, closing a loop in time."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure stated by the transmission, and of the kind that can be set up: the model predicts that a divination whose outcome is withheld from the diviner for good does worse than one whose outcome is later shown. It also explains in advance why a blinded protocol should fail, which makes it the first thing a test of the claim would have to separate. The short span and the minutes before the event are a further stated bound. Counted for the harvest: one failure condition, one stated span, no sign of success."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, p. 81; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicIncantatio a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment toward a future or a past event, and its limit"@en, "Verzauberung auf ein künftiges oder vergangenes Ereignis hin, und ihre Grenze"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity claimed: by establishing a future brain state that perceives a desired event as having happened, the magician gives physical reality a tendency to decohere toward a situation in which it has. The same mechanism is said to allow retroactive enchantment, which can undo the effects of past experiences on mind and body, with the limit that it cannot occur if it would change the measurable conditions of the present and so prevent itself, and that its effect can be measured only by how far the future deviates from its probable course."@en ;
    skos:note "The retroactive form is stated together with the only measure that would show it, and the measure is a counterfactual: the deviation of a future from a course that is not observed. That is the property a test would have to repair before it could begin, and it is the transmission that says so."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, pp. 81-82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmp:ChaosMagicIncantatioMemoriaFutura a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The spell stated as a memory of the accomplished event"@en, "Der Zauber als Erinnerung an das eingetretene Ereignis formuliert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The instruction drawn from the model: when enchanting for a future event, the magician is to establish, by visualisation coupled with gnosis, a future perception or memory of the event as having occurred, and not visualise the chain of events that would lead to it. A statement of intent in the form of a dated present, owning property at a named birthday, is preferred to a statement of will to become rich by that age, the first being said to engage the whole of entangled reality and the second only to improve one's choices."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, pp. 81-82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMemoriaFuturaYieldsIncantatio a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "The spell stated as accomplished is said to bend events toward it"@en, "Der als eingetreten formulierte Zauber soll die Ereignisse zu sich hinlenken"@de ;
    skos:definition "The work holds that the spell in the form of an accomplished perception, joined to gnosis, tends to bring reality to decohere toward the perceived state, and more strongly than a spell framed as a will toward a goal."@en ;
    skos:note "The comparison between the two forms of statement is the part of the claim that could be tested, since it predicts a difference between two procedures and not only an effect of one."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicIncantatioMemoriaFutura ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicIncantatio ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, pp. 81-82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

pmc:ChaosMagicNexusMagicus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The magical link as entanglement, with its carriers ranked"@en, "Die magische Verbindung als Verschränkung, mit gereihten Trägern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Entanglement is said to underlie the magical link and the old theories of magic by contagion, and decoherence to set severe limits on it. The carriers are ranked: simultaneous physical presence with physical or line-of-sight contact gives the best chance; artefacts once connected to the target, or remembered images, come second; photographs are a poor third; live images or a telephone call in real time may do better if they can be set up."@en ;
    skos:note "A ranked order is a claim that a series of trials could contradict, since it predicts the order of the outcomes and not only their occurrence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheApophenion ;
    dcterms:source "Carroll, The Apophenion: A Chaos Magic Paradigm (Mandrake of Oxford, 2008), chap. 5, Metadynamics, part 3, p. 82; read in a full-text layer of the first edition" .

#
# The Book of Results (Sherwin), harvested complete
#

pmw:TheBookOfResults a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "The Book of Results"@en, "The Book of Results"@de ;
    skos:note "Sherwin. First issued in 1978, as the first chapter says; the edition read carries a foreword by Peter Carroll calling it a fourth publication, and The Druid's Knot by Thessalonius Loyola, which its prefatory note says was unpublished until 1991. The full-text layer of the open scan archive carries no title page, imprint or page numbers, so the work is cited by chapter and heading. Opened and read complete on 2026-10-23, in two independent extraction passes and a reading of the whole text by the integrator."@en ;
    skos:note "Harvest counts: four signs of success (the recognised feeling of non-desiring, readiness to walk on coals, the vanishing of body awareness, seeing one's own body from behind) and eleven conditions of failure, of which one names an outcome the operator would notice, the feeling of foolishness after a rite done with unperfected technique, and one a duration, whirling under an hour. None names a deadline after which a sigil counts as having failed, and the record the work prescribes has no place for one."@en .

pmc:ChaosMagicBeliefAsCommodity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Belief as a changeable commodity, and the stillness from which one believes nothing"@en, "Glaube als austauschbare Ware, und die Stille, aus der man nichts glaubt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beliefs are held to be changeable commodities that the magician, and others, can manage and manipulate, not permanent concepts. Asked what he believes, the magician speaking from his central stillness should be able to answer honestly that he believes nothing, and from this blank slate adopt and discard beliefs as he sees fit. The way there is keeping oneself at the centre of one's own practice rather than taking over another's peculiarities, which also guards against picking up dogmas unawares; the basis of the scheme is called autopsy, strict and systematic self-enquiry, worked by the author into his translation of the Golden Verses of Pythagoras."@en ;
    skos:note "The example ritual applies the claim: the magician free of imposed belief has a clean slate, and adopts Brigid as his goddess until the termination of the rite (pmc:ChaosMagicBrigidNaming). The translation of the Golden Verses was not opened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDisengagingDisbelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The trained magician suspends disbelief at will, and sigilisation keeps proving the universe pliable"@en, "Der geschulte Magier setzt seinen Unglauben nach Belieben aus, und die Sigillenarbeit beweist die Formbarkeit der Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "As a result of his training the magician is said to be superbly capable of disengaging his disbelief, which he does every time he enters his temple and makes an invocation. A school of thought is reported, without a name, according to which the universe develops along the lines of consensus belief and grows ever less amenable to magic; constantly proving to oneself that the universe can be persuaded to behave otherwise is called a beneficial side issue of sigilisation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1, The Book of Results; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicReservoirOfConcentration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Habit frees a reservoir of unused concentration, and that reservoir is the key to magic"@en, "Gewohnheit setzt einen Vorrat ungenutzter Konzentration frei, und dieser Vorrat ist der Schlüssel zur Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Habit reduces the concentration a task needs, as grasping with the thumb and three-dimensional sight became habitual, and so releases the faculty of concentration for other uses; this now almost unused faculty, no longer called upon to maintain the organism, is called the key to the author's scheme of magic. Man is a lazy creature of habit who prefers comfort to adventure and stasis to motion, and only the greatest minds break out of it. The traditional method, by which the magician forced himself to do what his personality would put off, is said to have failed because it imposed new habits without stating its aims."@en ;
    skos:note "The failure of the traditional self-forcing is a stated condition of failure with its reason: an imposed discipline without a stated aim. The work's own remedy is the oath (pmp:ChaosMagicOath)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicCensorshipMechanism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The censorship mechanism that keeps one from performing to capacity"@en, "Der Zensurmechanismus, der einen hindert, nach Vermögen zu wirken"@de ;
    skos:definition "A censorship mechanism is said to prevent us from performing to capacity; whether it is taken for a function of the Holy Guardian Angel, a natural and necessary mental barrier, or the work of demons or aliens resident in the mind, the magician's objective is to bypass or destroy it, mapping his consciousness from within and wearing it down in proportion to his self-knowledge. In the model of the fourth chapter it is the third of six functions: anyone who has woken with a strong impression of a dream and no detail of it has seen it at work, refusing awareness and the willing part of the mind access to the night's activity, and it blocks information passing the other way in the same manner. Until personal equilibrium is reached it protects sanity by hiding an infinity of atavisms and the information accumulated in all the individual's lifetimes. It is to be observed and analysed, not developed."@en ;
    skos:note "The Holy Guardian Angel, demons and aliens are offered as other people's construals of the mechanism and are not named as beings the work holds to exist; no pm:Naming was entered for them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicWorkingToCapacity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Working to capacity: willed and necessary action only, and psychokinesis or telepathy as functions of it"@en, "Nach Vermögen wirken: nur gewollte und notwendige Handlung, und Psychokinese oder Telepathie als ihre Funktionen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The attainment the first chapter works toward: a mind cleared by the growth of licit and the dropping out of illicit activity, so that the magician performs willed and necessary actions and does not bend to the accretions of habit or appetite. Psychokinesis is offered as a possible function of the first category, that of necessity, and telepathy appears in the sixth: being no good telepath is no reason not to try, and perhaps succeed. Asked why he cannot perform psychokinesis, the magician must find the answer among seven: he does not believe it possible, has not spent enough time on it, does not believe it necessary, has better things to do, does not know where to begin, does not want to fail and so has not tried, or knows he can do it but has more important things to do."@en ;
    skos:note "Two conditions of failure are stated with the list. Disbelief in the possibility condemns the magician to his present level of performance, because he has not recognised that belief is arbitrary and can be changed like shoes; every other reason is the work of a demon, expelled by observation and confrontation. Neither is a sign by which a failed attempt could be recognised, and the list contains no answer of the form that the capacity does not exist: every outcome of an attempt is assigned in advance to the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis: the trancelike state that closes the analytical mind and the censorship mechanism"@en, "Gnosis: der tranceartige Zustand, der den analytischen Verstand und den Zensurmechanismus schließt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magical trancelike state brought about by excitation or by inhibition of the body. During it the mind is said to close the censorship mechanism as it closes the analytical functions, so that the sigil, together with the sensory data of the rite, colour, sound, smell and taste, is impressed onto the subconscious. The work likens it to the pain, fear or unconsciousness under which the dianetic model says an engram is laid down, and holds that magic is ineffective without the unconsciousness, self-inflicted and controlled, that every gnostic technique produces."@en ;
    skos:note "Sign of success, stated in the rubric of the action sigil: the gnostic state has been entered when awareness of the body disappears and the self is centred in, or wholly exterior to, the body. Counted as a sign and not as a condition of failure; the work names no sign by which an attempt at gnosis could be seen to have failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4, Action Sigils, Ritual Rubric; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPositiveNonDesiring a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Positive non-desiring, the state required for successful sigilisation"@en, "Positives Nichtbegehren, der für das Gelingen der Sigillenarbeit verlangte Zustand"@de ;
    skos:definition "A peculiar state of mind is called required for successful sigilisation, and paradoxically it is a state of non-desiring, likened to Crowley's no lust of result and to Spare's non-interest and neither-neither. The author denies that actively not desiring makes a result follow; positive non-desiring is only the optimum mode of operation, which he admits he can describe no better. Asked to explain it, he told a friend that he would identify the feeling when it arose; three years later the friend reported that he now knew exactly what was meant, and could find no better description either."@en ;
    skos:note "The only sign the work gives for the state is its recognition by the one who has it, and the only case reported took three years. Counted as a sign of success; it cannot discriminate, since the person who reports the feeling is the one told in advance that he will recognise it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicArrangedCoincidence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Arranged coincidence: the result of a sigil, from love and money to cosmic consciousness"@en, "Arrangierter Zufall: das Ergebnis einer Sigille, von Liebe und Geld bis zum kosmischen Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "The capacity the sigil is for. A sigil is a simple glyph representative of a particular or a general desire, and its uses are called innumerable: used over a period of time toward the loftiest aspirations, union with god or cosmic consciousness; in less spiritual endeavours toward astral exploration and exteriorisation; and on the mundane plane toward power, money or love. The result is to be recorded as to its nature, its extent and the time it came about; the sceptic calls it coincidence and the author arranged coincidence, which is magic. The limits of possibility are said to recede further as the magician becomes more adept."@en ;
    skos:note "The work sets those who call the use of sigils for gain black magic aside: morals are the operator's own concern. Entered at the capacity rather than as a dispute because the other side is named only as some of our less-evolved brethren and no work of theirs is cited."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExteriorisation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Exteriorisation, the out-of-body experience, as distinct from astral travel"@en, "Exteriorisation, die außerkörperliche Erfahrung, unterschieden von der Astralreise"@de ;
    skos:definition "Exteriorisation is the out-of-body experience, which the prefatory note to The Druid's Knot sets apart from astral travel and the other kinds of imaginary journeying. The poem holds the body to be rented space: the Self is not the body, is found wherever it cares to be, often in many places and times at once, and attaches to the body by interest, which is a yoke; when the body sleeps the Self plays where it pleases, and at death it loses interest entirely and goes. The eyes are warned against because they give the appearance that the Self is attached. In the fourth chapter magicians who have practised exteriorisation are said not to hold that the individual has many selves, since outside the body the picture is clearer; in the rubric of the action sigil the exterior position is called the ideal condition in which to create magical effects."@en ;
    skos:note "The prefatory note says the techniques of exteriorisation in the poem are intertwined with its other concepts and that anything out of the ordinary in its words and punctuation is a sign pointing away from where the words seem to go. The poem's procedures are entered at pmp:ChaosMagicTriangleOfMirrors and pmp:ChaosMagicRavineAsana; what else it may encode by that device was not read into it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4, Action Sigils, Ritual Rubric; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), The Druid's Knot, prefatory note and poem; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPresentTime a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Being in present time, in which obsession and the structure of the rite no longer bind"@en, "In gegenwärtiger Zeit sein, in der weder Besessenheit noch der Aufbau des Ritus binden"@de ;
    skos:definition "A banishing establishes a position in space and time, and the magician who is in present time is said to have no problems with obsession. The rubric of the action sigil returns to the term: in the absence of the individual Self one forgets that one is performing a ritual and is free to operate in present time, no longer concerned with or constrained by the structure of the rite, which is called what every practical ritualist seeks."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4, Action Sigils, Ritual Rubric; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicFirewalking a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The ability to walk on hot coals, and the readiness for it as the mark of the state"@en, "Die Fähigkeit, über glühende Kohlen zu gehen, und die Bereitschaft dazu als Zeichen des Zustands"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whirling is said to be most often used to induce the ability to walk on hot coals, and the feeling of being ready to do so is given as the sign that the desired state has been reached. Before whirling is used in ritual, setting up a firewalk is recommended as a way to demonstrate to oneself the effectiveness of the technique."@en ;
    skos:note "The one place in the work where a test is proposed by the work itself and has an outcome the operator does not decide: the coals burn or they do not. The sign given for the state is the operator's feeling of readiness, which is counted as a sign of success; the demonstration it recommends is the only procedure of the work whose failure would be visible to someone else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4, Action Sigils, Ritual Rubric; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicHealingPowers a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Amplified powers of healing, the aim of the example rite"@en, "Verstärkte Heilkräfte, das Ziel des Beispielritus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The aim chosen for the example of ritual sigilisation, chosen, the author says, to avoid glamourising the subject: to amplify the magician's powers of healing, framed in the accepted will sentence as being able to heal, not as becoming a healer."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3, An Example Ritual, preliminary considerations; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicAccessToTotalMemory a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The sub- or unconscious as a total record, accessible to whoever works at it"@en, "Das Unter- oder Unbewusste als vollständige Aufzeichnung, zugänglich für jeden, der daran arbeitet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fifth function of the model, the sub- or unconscious, is said to be the only part of the mind constantly vigilant and aware, even in deep sleep and unconsciousness, and a store of memory of inconceivable size: no experience, however often repeated or insignificant, goes unrecorded, and all the data about one's past are there and accessible to anyone prepared to work at accessing them. The second chapter calls it the brain that never sleeps and solves our problems while we believe ourselves asleep, and more effective in reshaping the subtle planes than the waking state with its constant internal dialogue; the ability to ignore the analytical mind and attend to its messages is called the quality we recognise as genius."@en ;
    skos:note "The censorship mechanism is said in the same model to hide the information gathered in all the individual's lifetimes, so the access claimed here reaches past this life; the work gives no procedure for it beyond working at it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicDianeticModel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The dianetic model of the mind, and disease as an accidentally created sigil"@en, "Das dianetische Modell des Geistes, und Krankheit als zufällig erzeugte Sigille"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dianetics is called one of the few non-magical disciplines to speculate on the innate powers of the mind, and its model is used positively. In unconsciousness or great emotional stress the analytical mind stops and a reactive mind records the impressions of the moment as engrams, which when restimulated cause disease and aberration. Magic deals with emotion, without which no force is generated, and in ritual with controlled unconsciousness; both states shut down the analytical mind and let data into the reactive mind. Disease and aberration are therefore uncontrolled restimulation of engrams, or accidentally created sigils. The negative example is a child struck by a car who, without recollection, later suffers severe headaches whenever some of the impressions of that second recur; the positive example is a sigil charged in a temple where every sensory input is relevant."@en ;
    skos:note "Reception, documented in the work: the model is taken over from the account of dianetics in Christopher Evans, Cults of Unreason, quoted at length, and not from a dianetic text. Evans is a modern secondary work and enters this record only as the place the author read the model; it was not opened."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSixMindFunctions a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Six functions of the mind attuned to chaos"@en, "Sechs Funktionen des Geistes, auf das Chaos abgestimmt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The model of the fourth chapter names six functions of the mind: S, Self; A, the part that reads, believes or disbelieves, wills and desires, egocentric and the home of the internal dialogue; B, awareness and perception; C, the censorship mechanism; D, gnosis, the method of bypassing C; and E, the sub- or unconscious. S, D and E are more attuned to chaos and unconditioned by consensus reality; all six relate to chaos to some degree, the magician develops four of them, Self is perfect and needs no development, and C is to be observed and analysed."@en ;
    skos:note "The model is declared by its author not to contain subtleties such as positive non-desiring nor to show anyone how to sigilise better; its function is to help the aspiring magician into the belief system."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicSingleSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "One Self, constant and unchanging, against the doctrine of many selves"@en, "Ein einziges Selbst, beständig und unveränderlich, gegen die Lehre von den vielen Selbsten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Self is said to have many functions but to be a constant, unchanging source that makes bodies as it chooses and is in itself unaffected by circumstance. The view, heard among magicians, that the individual is composed of many selves is called dangerous and unproductive on the evidence of the author's meditations and the practices that came from them; outside the body, Self is self and the source of self."@en ;
    skos:note "The foreword to the same edition marks the claim as contested inside the transmission: pmc:ChaosMagicSingleSelfDisputed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicChaosModel a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Order as a bar-code on chaos, and a universe that responds to perfect ritual"@en, "Ordnung als Strichcode auf dem Chaos, und eine Welt, die auf vollkommenes Ritual antwortet"@de ;
    skos:definition "The basis of the model is chaos, meaning the magician himself and the known and unknown universes seen without the point of view of order. Order is a convenience stamped on chaos like a bar-code, imposed so that we can function, communicate and socialise; consensus reality, demanding explainable cause and observable effect, ties itself in knots explaining strange events away. In the chaotic universe the magician creates and takes part in, everything is possible: the more successful his sigils, the more he understands that the cosmos is not rigid, and the more he sigilises the more he realises that, given optimum circumstances, which the text glosses as perfect ritual, the universe will respond to him."@en ;
    skos:note "The response of the universe is made conditional on perfect ritual, and the work gives no criterion for a perfect ritual other than its response; as stated, an unanswered rite is an imperfect one. The author also declines to explain how the sigil acts on chaos, because that way dogma lurks, while allowing each magician a satisfactory explanation of his own and pointing to subatomic physics and the butterfly of chaos theory."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicMethodWithoutBelief a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "A method that calls for no belief, held by an author who treats it as a belief system"@en, "Eine Methode, die keinen Glauben verlangt, gehalten von einem Autor, der sie als Glaubenssystem behandelt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The method of sigilisation is said to call for no belief or supposition and to need only adaptation to the working methods of whoever uses it; how to make sigils work is pragmatic, how they work speculative, and the speculation is kept to its own chapter. To know how a technique works can be an impediment while performing it in ritual, and yet a model of how it works acts almost as a sigil in itself. Magic requires a multi-model approach: one model means one approach, which restricts what information one receives, and whoever takes his magic from one source is prejudiced unless the source is his self. In the same chapter the author says he treats sigilisation itself as a belief system."@en ;
    skos:note "The two statements stand two pages apart and are not reconciled in the work: the method needs no belief, and the author holds it as one. Entered as found."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicExperimentalConfirmation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fifteen years of experiments said to confirm Spare's confidence in sigils"@en, "Fünfzehn Jahre Versuche, die Spares Zuversicht in die Sigillen bestätigen sollen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth chapter opens with the statement that Austin Osman Spare was emphatic about the workability of sigils and that the author's experiments over the last fifteen years, and those he persuaded other people to do, confirm Spare's confidence in the technique."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim of confirmation is given without a number of operations, a number of misses, a deadline or a description of a single result. The record the work prescribes would hold all of these (pmp:ChaosMagicBookOfResults); none is quoted from it. The foreword by Carroll adds that one can always tell the magicians who have understood Spare's mechanism from those who have not, which is the same kind of statement at one remove."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicRightMoment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Successful magic depends largely on the right moment, and the moment is the operator's feeling"@en, "Gelingende Magie hängt weitgehend vom richtigen Zeitpunkt ab, und der Zeitpunkt ist das Gefühl des Operators"@de ;
    skos:definition "Successful magic is said to depend to a great extent on having chosen the right moment. The traditional discussion of times by stars, moon and seasons is acknowledged, but the magician should choose the time he feels is best: in the example, Imbolc on the second of February as cognate with the scenario, the waxing moon as traditionally good for healing, or his birthday. Within the rite a second moment must be chosen, when during gnosis the sigil has become emblazoned on the non-analytical mind, and the sigil is destroyed as soon as possible after it."@en ;
    skos:note "Neither moment has a sign by which it could be recognised as wrong; both are left to the operator's judgement, so a failed rite can always be read back as a mistimed one."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3, An Example Ritual, preliminary considerations; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPersonalSymbolism a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Only personally vibrant symbols serve, and the subconscious, not a spirit, brings the result"@en, "Nur persönlich lebendige Symbole taugen, und das Unterbewusste, kein Geist, bringt das Ergebnis"@de ;
    skos:definition "A sigil is built according to whatever system of symbolism the magician finds most vibrant and effective. The medieval sigils of the planets, their spirits and intelligences remain useful only insofar as they can be built into a personal sigil the magician originates; there is no virtue in traditional ideas the operator finds glib, every aspect must be relevant from his point of view, and otherwise he wastes his time and energy, since it is his own subconscious and not some gaseous invertebrate that brings about the result. The same rule governs colour: red or orange for strength is traditional, through the fives of the tarot, Geburah and Mars, but if the strength one wants is perceived as blue one uses blue, and it is first-class magic to use the colours with the most striking effect on oneself."@en ;
    skos:note "A condition of failure with its reason: a sigil with elements the operator finds glib wastes time and energy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicTechniqueTrial a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Trying every technique, discarding the unsuitable and mastering the rest like callisthenics"@en, "Jede Technik erproben, die ungeeigneten verwerfen und die übrigen wie Gymnastik meistern"@de ;
    skos:definition "There are two basic kinds of magical technique, one that takes one into one's head and one that takes one out of it; whirling can do either according to intention, and drumming, drug-induced trance and some forms of mantra are gnostic techniques of the same kind. Techniques that inhibit the body, asana and sensory deprivation, suit looking inward, and those that excite it suit projecting outward. The individual should experiment with as many techniques as he can find or invent, at once discard those obviously unsuitable, and concentrate on mastering the rest; daily exercises need not be done in magical mood and may be treated like callisthenics or the practical forms of body yoga. Once mastered, a technique can be used confidently in ritual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCatalogueOfActivities a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The catalogue of activities: every action explained and assigned to one of seven categories"@en, "Der Katalog der Tätigkeiten: jede Handlung erklärt und einer von sieben Kategorien zugewiesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The first tactic against the censorship mechanism. The magician analyses every action he makes and explains to himself its reason, until his mind clears through the growth of licit and the dropping out of illicit activity. The reasons for doing or omitting anything are seven: necessity, health, welfare, income and development, which last is misleading and to be analysed ruthlessly; habit, from which he should desist whether or not it interferes with necessity; appetite, any activity whose only result is to stimulate the organism beyond the needs of necessity or nature; fear of the consequences of not acting; laziness, to which any of the others, income included, may also belong; unselfassuredness; and time measuring, activities that only pass time until something more important. Further reasons are bravado, pride, anxiety to please, ambition, the herd complex and stimulus-response."@en ;
    skos:note "The minute observation is said inevitably to make the magician do something he has meant to do for years, which the work counts a by-product unless that thing belongs to necessity. Not entered as a separate yielding: the work does not present it as an aim."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPersonalDemons a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Personifying the tricks of one's own mind as demons with names and sigils, and writing one's own grimoire"@en, "Die Kunstgriffe des eigenen Geistes als Dämonen mit Namen und Sigillen personifizieren und das eigene Grimoire schreiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since observation cannot be done in a vacuum, the easiest way for a magician skilled in conjuration and sigilisation to know and destroy the subtle tricks of the mind that keep him from working to capacity is to personify them as demons, each with its own name and sigil. A number of morning meditations produces the list of categories of action; licit activities need not be personified, all others are named and given a sigil, the names arbitrary or found by word-association. Then the magician observes their action. In keeping his record and identifying his demons he writes his own grimoire, exploring every trick of each demon in order to thwart it; it is easier by far to nullify a demon than to destroy a function the ego is anxious to keep, once disbelief has been suspended far enough to see the illicit actions as the machinations of something outside oneself."@en ;
    skos:note "Carroll's foreword reads the demons as very real personal blindspots, to be overcome by a daily regime of willed magical and material activity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1, The Book of Results; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBookOfResults a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Book of Results: the magical record as mirror, kept as scientifically as possible"@en, "Das Buch der Ergebnisse: die magische Aufzeichnung als Spiegel, so wissenschaftlich wie möglich geführt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The record is the magician's mirror, in which he sees himself as he is, his qualities and faults, his successes and failures meticulously recorded; its glory is not in success nor its shame in failure, but the glory is in works attempted and the shame in the unrecorded day. It is a permanent book, written carefully and illustrated where necessary, and the oath is copied into it. It is to be kept in as scientific a manner as possible: no experiment can be repeated exactly, since the heavens and the weather are beyond control, but the operator's own preparations, the time of day, the ritual trappings and his own state of mind are recorded as precisely as possible, and results as to their nature, their extent and the time at which they came about. When the first experiments are complete the record lets the magician see which methods were effective and discard the rest."@en ;
    skos:note "The one instrument in the work that could score a miss, and the work leaves it unarmed: results are recorded by nature, extent and time, but no deadline is set after which a sigil counts as having failed, and the operator is told to forget the sigil and its purpose after the rite (pmp:ChaosMagicForgettingTheSigil). A result can be entered; a non-result has no moment at which to be entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1, The Book of Results; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicOath a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The oath: a statement of needs, intentions and motives, not a pact"@en, "Der Eid: eine Erklärung der Bedürfnisse, Absichten und Beweggründe, kein Pakt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The oath is a statement and not a promise to or a pact with an imagined exterior power, however real that power may seem. It states the magician's needs, intentions and motives, tempered by his chosen strategy, and is changed or amended as he develops; explaining why it must change produces a clear restatement. It is not a task to be completed quickly. To be effective it must be both strict and encouraging, confining the magician to licit activity as he has defined it while permitting the broadening of his self-awareness and pointing him always in his chosen direction. Written and meditated upon to confirm its suitability, it is copied into the Book of Results and may be supplemented by a pantacle that incorporates its functions and their relations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1, The Oath; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicMagicalRetirement a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "A magical retirement of at least seven days on a fixed programme"@en, "Ein magischer Rückzug von mindestens sieben Tagen nach festem Programm"@de ;
    skos:definition "The inaugural phase of writing the grimoire is a full magical retirement to establish the new order, of at least seven days, called the minimum period for integrating a system in which each daily activity affects all the others. It begins with a fixed programme so that not an hour is wasted: acts of hygiene and necessity; pranayama three times a day, emphasised for its effects on self-discipline and clarity of thought; contemplation and writing up the record twice a day, analysing the previous day's activities and what should have been done and was not; physical exercise twice a day, since pranayama cannot be performed by an unhealthy person; meals as the oath prescribes; concentration on a chosen power twice a day; leisure; and sleep preceded by a mantra devised to exemplify the oath. Dreams may be recorded for analysis at the end. Afterwards the magician decides which activities to continue; the daily analyses and power concentration he must continue, and they grow harder as he re-enters mundane life."@en ;
    skos:note "Two stated bounds: seven days as the minimum for integration, and pranayama as impossible for an unhealthy person, which is why exercise stands in the programme. Neither carries a sign by which the retirement could be seen to have failed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1, The Daily Regime; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicGnosticTechniques a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The gnostic techniques: whirling to exhaustion, drumming, chanting, sex, sensory deprivation"@en, "Die gnostischen Techniken: Wirbeln bis zur Erschöpfung, Trommeln, Singen, Sexualität, Reizentzug"@de ;
    skos:definition "Having engineered his environment so that all sensory input is relevant, the magician raises his state of mind by whichever gnostic technique he finds most relevant: whirling to the point of exhaustion, drumming, chanting, sex, sensory deprivation, or any of the many others, drug-induced trance and mantra among them. The fourth chapter groups them as excitation or inhibition of the body."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 1; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 4; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSigilisation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sigilisation: desire acknowledged, contracted into a glyph, reified in gnosis and hurled into the subconscious"@en, "Sigillenarbeit: das Begehren anerkannt, in eine Glyphe zusammengezogen, in der Gnosis verdinglicht und ins Unterbewusste geschleudert"@de ;
    skos:definition "The cycle of action of a sigil always follows the same pattern. The magician acknowledges a desire and sets it down in symbols that depict and disguise it, as a sentence such as my desire is the keen eye of the hawk; a standing glyph may stand for the opening words that recur in most sigils. The rest is expressed pictographically and contracted, or, where pictures are impractical, the letters of the sentence are written down with duplicates omitted and moulded into an easily visualised glyph; other alphabets serve well. Colour may be added to the symbolism and as an aid to visualisation. Using any gnostic technique he reifies the sigil and by force of will hurls it into his subconscious, from where it works unencumbered by desire."@en ;
    skos:note "The temple of the positive example is set up both in the concrete world and in imagination, any shortfall in what is built being made up by imagination; the real temple contains nothing spurious to the idea of the temple needed (pmc:ChaosMagicSpuriousImpressionsCautela)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicFlashingColours a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Flashing colours: painting the sigil in complementaries so that it reappears in negative"@en, "Blitzende Farben: die Sigille in Komplementärfarben malen, damit sie im Negativ wiederkehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "To aid visualisation the sigil is painted in complementary or flashing colours, the detail in blue on an orange ground for instance, so that the adept concentrating on it receives an impression of flashing and on closing his eyes sees the whole in negative, the colours exchanged; a subliminal effect is also claimed. To find the complementary of a colour, paint it on paper, stare at it under bright light for half a minute and close the eyes. In the example rite the sigil is green, for healing, on red, its complementary, so that it appears green when the eyes close."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3, An Example Ritual, preliminary considerations; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicSigilMantra a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The mantra of the sigil, from the phonemes of the will sentence or as a spinning mantra"@en, "Das Mantra der Sigille, aus den Phonemen des Willenssatzes oder als Wirbelmantra"@de ;
    skos:definition "So that sight and hearing are both assailed by the postulate at the peak of gnosis, a mantra may accompany the sigil. It can be made like the alphabetic sigil from the phonemes of the will sentence, a method the author shows working badly on his own example sentence. His preferred form is the spinning mantra, most easily formed from foreign languages, of five lines of four syllables each with the stress on the second and fourth; energetically performed it produces a driving rhythm, is best chanted by two people taking alternate lines, and needs a great deal of practice. Containing none of the sense of the will sentence but the intention behind it, it is said to amplify the intention together with concentration on the sigil."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2; read in a full-text layer without page numbers", "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 2, An Example (mantra); read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicBanishing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing as capital letter and full stop of the rite"@en, "Das Bannen als Großbuchstabe und Schlusspunkt des Ritus"@de ;
    skos:definition "The banishing ritual is performed before and after the main body of a rite, first to clear the mind and extend its concentration for the rite, then to clear it of the rite, helping the magician adjust to the changes he has made in his personal reality and return to the mundane level: a capital letter and a full stop, with the rite as the sentence. Its form is best of the magician's own devising and consists in establishing a position in space and time; it may be done mentally or with full ceremonial trappings, which the author prefers, since the best symbol for a good sharp sword is a good sharp sword. The more a banishing is performed, the more effective it becomes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

pmp:ChaosMagicCaltropOfChaos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Caltrop of Chaos: a banishing that opens the mind to chaos instead of keeping it out"@en, "Der Krähenfuß des Chaos: ein Bannritual, das den Geist dem Chaos öffnet, statt es fernzuhalten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A banishing cum opening and closing, first published in the first issue of Chaos International, about ten minutes when decently paced and usable before most styles of rite since it relies on no angelic or divine form and no scenario. It rests on three assumptions: the phenomenal universe is best represented by the cardinal points, chaos by the caltropic points between them, which in the calendar are the times at which the energy of improbability is invited in, and black and white can be visualised without implying morality. Standing at the centre after deep, regular breathing, the magician declares the four forces, gravity, electromagnetism and the weak and strong forces, whose union in a big bang ends one universe and begins another; at each cardinal point he intones the mantra visualising a segment of white light rushing away from him; facing the first point he calls on chaos and cosmos to combine; after a pause and a second declaration he vibrates the mantra at each caltropic point visualising an inrushing black segment, the medium of improbability invited in, and calls again. At the end of the rite it is performed in reverse. The wedges are better imagined reaching to infinity above and below than on a flat plane."@en ;
    skos:note "The rite is set against the Golden Dawn banishing, whose function the author gives as a barrier against unknown energies, and against sophisticated magicians who spend their energy keeping out the chaos side of consciousness while shamans the world over go to great lengths to open themselves to it: without experience of chaos, the formative and usually hidden energy of the universe, one cannot use it for change. Not entered as a dispute, since no Golden Dawn text is cited and none was read here."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:TheBookOfResults ;
    dcterms:source "Sherwin, The Book of Results (an edition after 1991 with Carroll's foreword; no imprint in the layer read), chap. 3, The Caltrop of Chaos; read in a full-text layer without page numbers" .

